From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Jason Perlow <jperlow@gmail.com>
Date: Mon, 20 Jul 2026 13:05:00 +0800
Subject: [PATCH] soc: cix: acpi-resource-lookup: validate RSTL entries +
 add ACPI-name fallback

Two coupled fixes for Orion O6N (CIX Sky1, ACPI) in the v7.0.12/stable
tree; mirrors the v7.2/ncz tree's behaviour.

1. Tighten the RSTL entry guard in reset_lookup_handle() to match
   reset core's actual contract: drivers/reset/core.c rejects entries
   with !dev_id || !provider.  The CIX RSTL parser used a different
   condition (admitted NULL dev_id whenever con_id was non-NULL),
   which let three unusable entries slip through to reset core, which
   then emitted "reset lookup entry badly specified, skipping" three
   times per boot on O6N.

2. Add the ACPI-name fallback into acpi_obj_to_devname() that already
   exists in the v7.2/ncz tree.  Without it, any RSTL entry whose
   consumer's platform_device has not been instantiated yet at
   subsys_initcall time (e.g. usb_reset/usb_preset on CIXH2030/CIXH2032
   USB PHY/controller) gets dev_id == NULL and is now silently dropped
   by the tightened guard from (1), regressing devm_reset_control_get()
   resolution.  The fallback uses acpi_dev_name(adev) -- for
   ACPI-enumerated platform devices, dev_name(dev) equals the ACPI
   device's bus id, so the lookup remains matchable.

Replace the dropped entries with a dev_dbg diagnostic so future debug
runs can see which entry was rejected and why (provider/dev_id/con_id
strings), instead of just "badly specified".

The v7.2/ncz tree (drivers/soc/cix/acpi/cix-acpi-resource-lookup-v1.c)
already has the fallback; this patch only adds the guard tightening
there.  Same fix class as the night's -ENOENT-to-EPROBE_DEFER clock
fixes -- O6N-specific, not a generic kernel bug.

Signed-off-by: Jason Perlow <jperlow@gmail.com>
---
--- a/drivers/soc/cix/cix-acpi-resource-lookup.c
+++ b/drivers/soc/cix/cix-acpi-resource-lookup.c
@@ -53,15 +53,48 @@
 	return bus_find_device_by_acpi_dev(&platform_bus_type, adev);
 }
 
+static struct acpi_device *acpi_obj_to_acpi_dev(const union acpi_object *obj)
+{
+	if (!obj)
+		return NULL;
+
+	if (obj->type == ACPI_TYPE_LOCAL_REFERENCE)
+		return acpi_fetch_acpi_dev(obj->reference.handle);
+
+	if (obj->type == ACPI_TYPE_STRING) {
+		acpi_handle handle;
+		acpi_status status;
+
+		status = acpi_get_handle(NULL, obj->string.pointer, &handle);
+		if (ACPI_FAILURE(status))
+			return NULL;
+		return acpi_fetch_acpi_dev(handle);
+	}
+
+	return NULL;
+}
+
 static const char *acpi_obj_to_devname(const union acpi_object *obj)
 {
+	struct acpi_device *adev;
 	struct device *dev;
 
 	dev = acpi_obj_to_platform_device(obj);
-	if (!dev)
-		return NULL;
+	if (dev)
+		return dev_name(dev);
 
-	return dev_name(dev);
+	/*
+	 * The target platform_device may not be instantiated yet when this
+	 * lookup runs (subsys_initcall ordering vs ACPI platform-device
+	 * creation).  For ACPI-enumerated platform devices, dev_name() equals
+	 * the ACPI device's bus id, so fall back to that deterministic name.
+	 * Without this, reset_control lookups for not-yet-probed consumers
+	 * (e.g. usb_reset/usb_preset on CIXH2030/CIXH2032) get a NULL dev_id
+	 * and the tightened guard below would silently drop them, leaving
+	 * USB PHY/controller resets unresolved.  Mirrors the v7.2/ncz tree.
+	 */
+	adev = acpi_obj_to_acpi_dev(obj);
+	return adev ? acpi_dev_name(adev) : NULL;
 }
 
 /*
@@ -111,8 +144,18 @@
 			devm_kstrdup(dev, el[3].string.pointer, GFP_KERNEL)
 			: NULL;
 
-		if (!lookup->provider || (!lookup->dev_id && !lookup->con_id))
+		/*
+		 * reset_controller_add_lookup() requires both fields, and lookup
+		 * matching starts with dev_id before considering con_id.  The ACPI
+		 * name fallback above covers consumers not instantiated yet, so a
+		 * missing name here denotes an unresolved firmware reference.
+		 */
+		if (!lookup->provider || !lookup->dev_id) {
+			dev_dbg(&adev->dev,
+				"invalid RSTL[%d]: provider[%s] dev[%s] con[%s]\n",
+				i, lookup->provider, lookup->dev_id, lookup->con_id);
 			continue;
+		}
 
 		reset_controller_add_lookup(lookup, 1);
 

-- 
